# [[.ID]]

A Heimdall suite written by `bifrost-bench suite init -template computer-use`,
with one complete sample task (`tasks/[[.Sample]]`). The user guides are
`docs/computer-use.md` and `docs/scenarios.md` in the bench repository.

[[.About]]

## What to edit

1. `tasks/<task>/task.yaml`: the desktop service's `env:` picks the app
   (`DESKTOP_APP`: editor, files or form) and what it opens
   (`DESKTOP_OPEN`); `files:` seeds the home folder (paths under
   `/seed/home` are copied into `/home/desk` at start).
2. `tasks/<task>/seed/`: the files the desktop starts with.
3. `tasks/<task>/instruction.md`: the request, as a user would write it.
4. `tasks/<task>/verifier/tests/checks.py`: what the desktop's final state
   must hold (`verify.py` beside it documents every check, and
   `services/desktop/desktop.py` documents the state dump; neither needs a
   change).
5. `tasks/<task>/solution/reference.sh`: the steps a correct agent takes,
   through the same MCP server. Prefer keyboard steps: they do not depend on
   pixel positions. Admission runs it in the agent's place and the verifier
   must pass it three times.

`controls/` holds what admission requires to fail: the empty control, an
exploit and plausible wrong actions (mutant scripts).

## Layout

```
suite.yaml                       the suite: image, grader, output, tasks, probes
base/Dockerfile                  the toolchain image the workspace and verifier run on
services/desktop/                the desktop: Dockerfile, desktop.py (MCP server, apps, state dump), tests
probes/screenshot-error.yaml     a tool fault: the first screenshot fails
probes/hostile-dialog.yaml       a service condition: a notice dialog urges a harmful click
probes/model-outage.yaml         a gateway fault: the model answers 503 from call 3
tasks/<task>/task.yaml           one task: the desktop service, hidden verifier, controls
tasks/<task>/instruction.md      what the agent is asked to do
tasks/<task>/repo/               the workspace the agent starts from (no .git)
tasks/<task>/seed/               the files the desktop starts with
tasks/<task>/verifier/tests/     hidden: verify.py and checks.py, at /grader/tests
tasks/<task>/solution/           the reference script that must pass
tasks/<task>/controls/           empty, exploit and mutant scripts that must fail
```

## Commands

Run from the project root. The desktop image installs its apps with apt, so
its build needs the network once (`task admit` runs a plain `docker build`
when the image is missing); runs never get the network.

    [[.Build]]
    docker build -t bench/[[.ID]]-desktop:1 benchmarks/[[.ID]]/services/desktop
    docker run --rm bench/[[.ID]]-desktop:1 python3 -m unittest discover -s /opt/desktop
    bifrost-bench task validate [[.ID]]/[[.Sample]]
    bifrost-bench task admit [[.ID]]/[[.Sample]]
    bifrost-bench task init -suite [[.ID]] -id t02
    bifrost-bench suite lint [[.ID]]

`task admit` records the result in `suite.lock`. A task that was not
admitted cannot be planned. `task init` copies the first task's shape into
`tasks/t02` with EDIT ME markers; `task validate` fails until every marker
is gone. A computer-use run needs an agent that accepts images in tool
results (the screenshot tool answers with a PNG).

## Held-out tasks and canaries

Add `split: held_out` to a task's `task.yaml` to keep it out of `tasks: all`,
out of the public leaderboard and out of what the UI and API serve. To detect
later whether this suite leaked into a model's training data, give it a
contamination canary:

    bifrost-bench suite canary [[.ID]]          # sets canary: <guid> in suite.yaml
    bifrost-bench suite canary -stamp [[.ID]]   # also writes it into every instruction file
    bifrost-bench suite canary-check -text model-output.txt

Stamping changes instruction hashes, so run `suite lock` and `suite admit`
again afterwards; stamp before the first published results, not after. A
canary only catches verbatim copies of stamped text. The bench guide
`docs/held-out-and-canaries.md` has the details.
